# Drop-off Location Finder – UI Implementation Rule

## Objective

Build the complete UI for the Drop-off Location Finder, allowing merchants to search, view, and confirm drop-off locations for their shipments.  
**No backend logic or API integration—focus is on layout, navigation, and user experience.**

---

## 1. Technology & Design System

- **Framework:** Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- **Language:** TypeScript
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS only
- **UI Components:** shadcn/ui (https://ui.shadcn.com/)
- **State Management:** Zustand (if needed)
- **Transitions:** Use premium, subtle transitions as per premium-transitions-and-effects.mdc
- **Accessibility:** All UI must be accessible (ARIA, keyboard navigation, color contrast)
- **Naming & Scoping:** All new UI features, pages, and components **must** follow the rules in `@naming-and-scoping.mdc` for unique IDs, class names, and structure. This is required for easy inspection and debugging.

---

## 2. Scope & Requirements

### Drop-off Location Finder Flow (All UI, No Backend)
- **Find Drop-off Location Page:**
  - Google Maps UI placeholder (no real API calls)
  - Search bar for location/address
  - List of mock drop-off locations (name, address, hours, distance)
  - Location cards with details and "Select" button
  - Map marker icons for each location
  - Option to filter by open/closed, distance, or type
  - Responsive design for mobile and desktop

- **Drop-off Confirmation:**
  - Confirmation UI after selecting a location
  - Show selected location details
  - "Confirm Drop-off" button
  - Success message and navigation back to dashboard

---

## 3. Navigation

- Use Next.js App Router for all navigation.
- "Find Drop-off Location" button on dashboard or post-purchase must route to this flow.
- All navigation and state changes handled client-side (no server calls).

---

## 4. Data & Logic

- All data is static, mocked, or placeholder.
- No API calls, no backend logic, no authentication.
- Use mock data for locations, hours, and status.

---

## 5. Code Quality & Structure

- Use TypeScript everywhere.
- Functional components only.
- Early returns, no dead code, no TODOs.
- Descriptive naming and clear structure.
- All forms, buttons, and flows must be visually complete and accessible.
- **All new UI features, pages, and components must follow the rules in `@naming-and-scoping.mdc` for IDs and class names.**

---

## 6. Deliverables

- All pages and components for the drop-off location finder flow.
- Fully navigable UI demo (no backend).
- README update with instructions for running and testing the drop-off flow.

---

## 7. Enforcement

- Code reviews must verify adherence to this rule file, `@naming-and-scoping.mdc`, and all referenced standards.
- Any deviation must be documented and approved by the project lead.

---

**Goal:**  
Deliver a pixel-perfect, accessible, and modern drop-off location finder UI, ready for future backend/API integration.

